# Developing GTKX Applications

GTKX renders React components as native GTK4 widgets through a Rust FFI bridge.

## Quick Start

```tsx
// src/index.tsx
import { GtkApplication, GtkApplicationWindow, GtkBox, GtkButton, render, quit } from "@gtkx/react";
import * as Gtk from "@gtkx/gi/gtk";

const App = () => (
    <GtkApplication applicationId="com.example.myapp">
        <GtkApplicationWindow title="My App" defaultWidth={800} defaultHeight={600} onClose={quit}>
            <GtkBox orientation={Gtk.Orientation.VERTICAL} spacing={12}>
                Hello, GTKX!
                <GtkButton label="Quit" onClicked={quit} />
            </GtkBox>
        </GtkApplicationWindow>
    </GtkApplication>
);

render(<App />);
```

`render` is called once at module top-level — analogous to `createRoot().render()` in `react-dom`. In dev, `gtkx dev` restarts the Node process whenever a non-component file changes, so this top-level call only ever runs once per process life.

## Essential Patterns

### Layout

```tsx
<GtkBox orientation={Gtk.Orientation.VERTICAL} spacing={12}>
    <GtkLabel label="Title" />
    <GtkButton label="Click" onClicked={handleClick} />
</GtkBox>
```

### Controlled Input

```tsx
const [text, setText] = useState("");
<GtkEntry text={text} onChanged={(e) => setText(e.getText())} />
```

### Signals

GTK signals map to `on<SignalName>` props: `clicked` → `onClicked`, `toggled` → `onToggled`.

### Widget Slots

Some widgets require children in specific slots. The idiomatic form is a prop on the widget; the compound wrapper synthesizes the underlying `<Slot>` for you:

```tsx
<GtkPaned startChild={<Sidebar />} endChild={<Content />} />
```

When no compound prop fits, fall back to the bare `<Slot>` intrinsic (no import needed — it is a JSX intrinsic, and `id` is the only prop):

```tsx
<GtkPaned>
    <Slot id="startChild"><Sidebar /></Slot>
    <Slot id="endChild"><Content /></Slot>
</GtkPaned>
```

### Container Slots (HeaderBar, ActionBar, ToolbarView, ActionRow, ExpanderRow)

Packing slots are also plain props on the compound widget (`packStart=`, `packEnd=`, `addTopBar=`, `addPrefix=`, …):

```tsx
<GtkHeaderBar
    packStart={<GtkButton iconName="go-previous-symbolic" />}
    packEnd={<GtkMenuButton iconName="open-menu-symbolic" />}
/>
```

The bare `<ContainerSlot>` intrinsic is the escape hatch — its `id` is the parent method name used to append the child:

```tsx
<GtkHeaderBar>
    <ContainerSlot id="packStart"><GtkButton iconName="go-previous-symbolic" /></ContainerSlot>
    <ContainerSlot id="packEnd"><GtkMenuButton iconName="open-menu-symbolic" /></ContainerSlot>
</GtkHeaderBar>
```

### Animations

Animations are first-class components, picked by physics model: `AdwSpringAnimation` (damping/stiffness/mass) or `AdwTimedAnimation` (duration/easing). Import the name from `@gtkx/react`.

```tsx
<AdwSpringAnimation
    initial={{ opacity: 0, scale: 0.8 }}
    animate={{ opacity: 1, scale: 1 }}
    damping={0.8}
    stiffness={200}
    animateOnMount
>
    <GtkLabel label="Animated!" />
</AdwSpringAnimation>
```

```tsx
<AdwTimedAnimation
    initial={{ opacity: 0 }}
    animate={{ opacity: 1 }}
    duration={300}
    easing={Adw.Easing.EASE_OUT_CUBIC}
    animateOnMount
>
    <GtkLabel label="Fade in" />
</AdwTimedAnimation>
```

## Key Constraints

- GTK is single-threaded: all widget operations on main thread
- GtkEntry requires two-way binding with `onChanged`
- Virtual lists need stable object references (immutable data patterns)
- Use `quit` from `@gtkx/react` to close the application

